}

```
This would output all "Debug.Assert(...);" statements from the specified C# file. 

Please replace `"yourFile.cs"` with the path to the `.cs` file you want to analyze. The regular expression matches the "Debug.Assert(...);" statement, which can be any string of characters inside the "(" and ")" braces.

Please note that you need additional lines to get this code to work, like `using System.IO;` and `using System.Text.RegularExpressions;`, etc. Those lines are added just to make sure the Regex and File I/O parts of the code work. Also note that the regular expression might not work perfectly in all situations, and you might need to adjust the regular expression to suit your specific requirements. 

You may also want to implement it in a method where you can execute it to get your output for better readability. Also, you might want to use Debug.Assert statements for testing in situations where you want to verify certain conditions, and in those cases, Debug.Assert is generally used to do so. If you're dealing with regular expressions, the Debug.Assert statements are not likely used in your code.--------------------Using regular expressions in both C# and .NET to find all instances of a regular expression pattern. The example below is in C# using the Regular expressions namespace. The regular expression `@"\/\/\s+@@\@(.+)" ` will be used to find all lines starting with "// @@@ "

```cs
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string input = "Your original C# code string";

        string pattern = @"\/\/\s+@@\@(.+)";
        
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(input, pattern);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Groups[1].Value.TrimStart());
        }
    }
}
```

If you are running this code as an application, substitute the input variable with your C# code. The regular expression is saying to look for a line that starts with "// @@@" (the "//" characters are C# comment symbols). We are then using the Groups property of the Match object to extract the content after the "@@@" comment identifier.

Remember that the regular expression above uses @ character at start of the string due to which it expects the string to be treated as a verbatim string. If you use regular string then just remove @ symbols from start of regular expression. 

Replace the `Your original C# code string` with your actual C# code. This code will output the C# code corresponding to the assertion statements in the comments. The output will be printed in the console.--------------------The expected output is a string representation of the csharp code that is meant to be put into the csharp codebase to facilitate a testing environment and to be executed to validate the correctness of the program. However, due to the complexity and specificity of this code and its comments, the output would be very specific and may vary from the given example. 

Here is the string that represents the code that asserts that the return value of the `Contains` method is true, the new list's last index of x is not less than or equal to the old list's last index of x. 

```csharp

Debug.Assert(New_Ret && New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

```

However, keep in mind that the exact values used here and the comments "old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x" and "new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x" are placeholders or names given for these variables to make the code more understandable. The actual names used in your C# code should be substituted for the placeholders here.--------------------Here is the CSharp code corresponding to the comments in the given C# code. Please replace the "ArrayList arrList, int x" with the actual data type and variable of your ArrayList and x. The "Debug.Assert" statements will check the conditions stated in the comments and throw an exception if they are not met.

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    using System.Diagnostics;

    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            // @@@ If the return value is true, new list's last index of x is >= old list's last index of x
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret, "Method 'arrList.Contains(x)' returned false.");
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "new list's last index of x is not >= old list's last index of x");
        }
    }
}

```

Here, the first Debug.Assert (line 31) checks if the Contains method returns 'false', which may be the case if the list does not contain the element 'x'. The second Debug.Assert (line 32) checks if 'New_arrListLastIndexOfX' is less than or equal to 'Old_arrListLastIndexOfX', which means if the element 'x' was not found in the old list, it should not be found in the new list either. If either of these conditions is not met, an exception will be thrown.--------------------In C# programming language, there isn't a built-in function to assert the expression like in Java. 
But we can use Debug.Assert() method for that purpose. You can read more about it [here](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.debug?view=net-5.0).

Also, I'm assuming that the question asked is about generating Debug.Assert() statements from a given method in order to verify post-conditions.

Here's an example of how this could look for this specific scenario:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount, "The Count property of the list should not change.");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX, "The IndexOf method should return a valid index or -1.");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "The LastIndexOf method should return a valid index or -1.");
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == arrList.Contains(x), "The Contains method should return a valid result.");
```

Each of these lines will generate an assertion statement for the corresponding condition, if the condition is false the debugger will break execution and display a message. The text after the comma in these statements will be displayed as the error message if the assertion fails.

Please note that these messages won't be shown if the program is running in a release configuration but rather shown in a debug build specifically. They can also only be viewed if you have Visual Studio debug configuration with Pex Visualizing settings enabled.--------------------